Yes. Match the operator, not necessarily the move.

If someone is exploiting opportunity cost, the counter is to stop evaluating choices in isolation. Every “yes” consumes something elsewhere: time, attention, optionality, credibility, capital, cognitive bandwidth. So make the comparison explicit:

Value(move) = expected gain − direct cost − best foregone alternative − loss of optionality.

Then apply it recursively. If responding to something costs us more than ignoring it, don’t respond. If delaying a commitment preserves several better branches, delay. If a cheap reversible probe can buy information before an expensive commitment, probe first. If somebody wants our attention, make that attention compete against everything else we could be doing with it.

And this meshes perfectly with the commit membrane we just captured:

cheap exploration → preserve options → gather information → compare branches → commit only when the opportunity cost is justified.

The nastier strategic inversion is also useful: don’t merely minimize our own opportunity cost; create positive alternatives so compelling that the wrong path becomes expensive by comparison. You often don’t need to block a route when you can make another route obviously more valuable.

That is opportunity cost used offensively without becoming destructive: expand our option set faster than circumstances can narrow it.